      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but I want to get even more specific for our marketing and product positioning teams. \n\nIf we're looking at the way we present the app's core features—like investment tools or high-yield savings—should we adjust the tone of our copy and the complexity of the data for these groups? For example, for Hiroshi, are we assuming he needs a high-density, data-heavy interface because of that \"precision and efficiency\" mindset? \n\nAnd on the flip side, for Amara or Omar, would it be better to use more storytelling-driven, approachable language? I'm trying to figure out if we should be designing to a \"high-tech/rational\" archetype for the Japanese segment vs. a more \"vibrant/community-driven\" archetype for the Nigerian or Middle Eastern segments. Does that distinction accurately reflect how these markets actually behave, or am I over-indexing on certain cultural tropes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
